 # S-Shaped Coaxial Dilators
 
-**Double-curve ("S-shaped") urethral dilators passed over a pre-placed guidewire** — the engineering compromise between the **anatomical conformity of the curved [Van Buren](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/van-buren) sound** and the **safety of guidewire-assisted dilation** historically delivered by straight coaxial dilators or the [Balloon Dilator](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/balloon-dilator). The S-shape matches the natural double curve of the male urethra (pendulous urethra curving inferiorly, then bulbar urethra curving superiorly around the perineal body); the coaxial track over a guidewire constrains the dilator to the true lumen and **eliminates the principal complication of blind metal-bougie dilation — false-passage creation**. Originally described by **Herschorn and Carrington (2007)**.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
+S-shaped coaxial dilators are hydrophilic urethral dilators advanced over a **confirmed intravesical guidewire**. Their curved profile is designed for the male urethral course while the wire maintains access across a narrowing. Wire guidance can reduce the risk of losing the true lumen, but it does **not eliminate** false passage, perforation, bleeding, or recurrence.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
 
-## Design and Rationale
+## Current Device Configuration
 
-- **Double-curve (S-shaped) profile** along the working length — matches the pendulous → bulbar urethral curvature without the blind manipulation required by the single-curve Van Buren.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
-- **Coaxial central lumen** for over-the-wire passage — designed for a standard 0.035" guidewire (PTFE-coated or hydrophilic Glidewire) pre-placed across the stricture under cystoscopic or fluoroscopic vision.
-- **French sizing** in sequential graduated increments, allowing stepwise dilation from baseline caliber to the therapeutic endpoint.
-- **Single-instrument-per-size** simplicity — no threaded follower assembly as with [filiform and follower](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments) systems.
+For the current Cook S~Curve family:<sup>[[2]](#ref2)[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
 
-The Herschorn / Carrington design emerged from **prototype iteration** to settle on the S-curve geometry and insertion technique that allowed smooth passage from meatus to bladder neck in routine practice.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
+- Intended for dilation of the male urethra.
+- AQ hydrophilic coating; the IFU directs pre-soaking in sterile water or isotonic saline.
+- 37-cm dilators with a sideport.
+- Set sizes 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 18, and 20 Fr; 22, 24, and 26 Fr are listed as individual dilators.
+- Compatible with a **0.038-inch guidewire**.
 
-## Reconstructive-Urology Uses
+These are manufacturer-specific facts, not universal specifications for every curved coaxial dilator.
 
-- **Recurrent bulbar or pan-urethral stricture** requiring dilation when blind metal-bougie passage has previously created false passage or carries high risk.
-- **Anterior-urethral dilation in the patient with prior failed DVIU** where preservation of the true lumen is critical for the next intervention.
-- **Pediatric urethral dilation** — guidewire-assisted dilation is particularly safe in pediatric patients (Dewan 2003: **32 pediatric cases, no false passages, no sepsis**), and the S-shape adapts the same advantage to the adult urethra.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
-- **As a bridge to definitive urethroplasty** in the symptomatic patient awaiting reconstruction.
-- **Catheter exchange over wire** through a difficult-to-access stricture — the S-shape navigates the urethra without re-instrumenting blindly.
+## Rationale and Evidence
 
-For full procedural context, see [DVIU and Urethral Dilation](/docs/surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/dviu).
+Herschorn and Carrington described prototype development and a final S-shaped design intended to facilitate male urethral stricture dilation while minimizing trauma. The publication is a short device/technique report; it does not establish superiority over balloons, straight coaxial dilators, or sounds in a randomized comparison.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
 
-## Comparison of Urethral Dilation Methods
+In a pediatric series, guidewire-and-sheath dilation was performed under cystoscopic guidance in 32 patients. Three developed UTI and one bladder spasms; no false passage or sepsis occurred in that series. Those data support verified wire-guided dilation in that population, not automatic extrapolation of one adult S-dilator product to all pediatric strictures.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup>
 
-| Feature | **S-shaped coaxial** | [**Van Buren**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/van-buren) | **Straight coaxial** | **Filiform + followers** | [**Balloon dilator**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/balloon-dilator) |
-|---|---|---|---|---|---|
-| **Curvature** | **Double curve (S)** matching pendulous + bulbar | Single distal J-curve | Straight | Flexible filiform tip | N/A (inflatable balloon) |
-| **Guidewire-guided** | **Yes** | No (blind) | Yes | No (threaded coaxial assembly) | Yes |
-| **Conforms to male urethra** | **Excellent** | Good | Poor | Variable | N/A |
-| **False-passage risk** | Low — wire-constrained | Higher — blind, rigid | Low — wire-constrained, but fights the curve | Moderate | **Lowest** (direct vision) |
-| **Force mechanism** | Axial (shearing) | Axial (shearing) | Axial (shearing) | Axial (coaxial) | **Radial** |
-| **Setup overhead** | Single instrument over wire | Single instrument blind | Single instrument over wire | Multi-component threaded assembly | Wire + balloon + inflator |
+## Uses
 
-The defining advantages over the Van Buren are **guidewire-tracked safety** and **better anatomic fit**. The defining advantage over the straight coaxial dilator is **anatomic conformity** — the straight coaxial fights the bulbar curve and demands more axial force.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup> The defining advantage over the [balloon dilator](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/balloon-dilator) is **mechanical simplicity and lower cost**; the balloon retains the advantage of radial-vs-axial force and direct-vision dilation.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
+- Selected male urethral strictures that admit a guidewire and require sequential dilation.
+- Difficult catheter placement when verified wire access has been obtained and dilation is needed to place the drainage catheter.
+- Temporary drainage or symptom management when definitive reconstruction is not immediately performed, with recurrence counseling and a disease-specific plan.
 
-## Technique
+The instrument is not a substitute for assessing stricture length, location, etiology, prior treatment, spongiofibrosis, or candidacy for urethroplasty.
 
-1. **Cystoscopic or fluoroscopic guidewire placement** across the stricture into the bladder. The AUA 2023 guideline explicitly notes that urethral strictures may be **dilated over a guidewire to prevent false-passage formation** — Freid and Smith's Glidewire technique is the standard.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
-2. **Sequential dilation** — pass S-shaped dilators in ascending French sizes over the wire, oriented so the S-curve tracks the pendulous → bulbar bend.
-3. **Catheter placement** — after the desired caliber is achieved, a Foley can be passed over the same guidewire to maintain access.
-4. **Antibiotic prophylaxis** — standard for any urethral instrumentation.
+## Technique and Verification
 
-## Safety Profile
+1. Define the obstruction and stop repeated blind attempts when bleeding, severe pain, or a false passage is suspected.
+2. Place the compatible guidewire through the true lumen into the bladder under direct vision or fluoroscopy whenever possible.
+3. **Confirm intravesical position before dilation.** The AUA difficult-catheter lesson describes confirmation with urine drainage through an open-ended catheter or an 8-Fr S-dilator, cystoscopic/contrast confirmation, or ultrasound evidence appropriate to the setting.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
+4. Prepare the hydrophilic surface exactly as the IFU specifies; for S~Curve, soak in sterile water or isotonic saline.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
+5. Advance sequential sizes over the wire without forcing resistance. The AUA lesson describes the S-dilator sequence from 8 to 20 Fr and, for catheter drainage, dilation only **2–4 Fr beyond the intended catheter**; a 14-Fr Council-tip catheter is often sufficient.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
+6. Maintain wire control while removing each dilator. Before passing a Council-tip catheter, reconfirm access if wire position is uncertain.
+7. Choose catheter size, duration, culture management, and antimicrobial prophylaxis from the clinical procedure and current guidance—not from a blanket rule attached to the dilator.
 
-- **False passage** — markedly reduced vs blind metal-bougie dilation because the dilator is constrained to the wire-defined true lumen.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
-- **Urethral hemorrhage, UTI, bacteremia** — standard urethral-instrumentation risks; comparable to other dilation modalities.
-- **Stricture recurrence** — the underlying biologic problem (mucosal rupture → fibroblast activation) is not solved by changing the instrument; durability remains determined by stricture length, etiology, and prior treatment burden. Balloon dilation has been proposed to improve healing via **radial rather than axial force**, but published data have not shown a clear long-term recurrence advantage over sound-based methods.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
+Freid and Smith's small series illustrates the confirmation principle: a hydrophilic wire was passed like a filiform, intravesical position was confirmed with a ureteral catheter, and the wire was exchanged before dilation or catheter placement.<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
 
-## Current Practice Context
+## Comparison Boundaries
 
-Despite the theoretical advantages of guidewire-assisted dilation, **blind metal-bougie dilation remains the majority practice**: the Kaçtan 2026 Turkish urology survey found **47% blind metal-bougie**, **23% plastic dilator over guidewire**, and **26% disposable catheter** — with **non-metal, guidewire-based methods significantly more common in academic centers** (p = 0.04).<sup>[[7]](#ref7)</sup> The **2026 EAU guideline** emphasizes that single-session dilation offers outcomes similar to DVIU with **reduced complications when visually controlled**.<sup>[[8]](#ref8)</sup>
+| Method | Access feature | Important limitation |
+|---|---|---|
+| **S-shaped coaxial dilator** | Tracks over a guidewire; curved profile for male urethra | Requires verified wire access; dilation still disrupts scar and may recur |
+| [**Van Buren sound**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/van-buren) | Rigid curved instrument without a wire lumen | Blind passage can create or extend a false passage |
+| **Straight coaxial dilator** | Tracks over a guidewire | Device geometry and operator technique differ; no universal evidence that one shape is clinically superior |
+| [**Filiform + followers**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/filiforms-followers) | Narrow filiform leader with threaded followers | Blind leader passage requires objective confirmation before dilation |
+| [**Balloon dilator**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/balloon-dilator) | Radial expansion over verified access | Product-specific pressures, dimensions, and indications; not inherently risk-free |
 
-## Limitations
+Avoid simple “low/lowest risk,” “best fit,” or cost rankings unless a specific comparative study and device are named. Instrument choice depends on anatomy, access confirmation, operator experience, available visualization, and the immediate goal.
 
-- **Does not address the fibrotic substrate** — like all axial-shearing dilators, the S-shaped coaxial stretches and ruptures scar; recurrence remains the limiting factor in long, dense, or radiation-induced strictures.
-- **Requires a guidewire to be passable** — in a completely obliterated stricture, a filiform-and-follower system or open exploration may still be necessary before any coaxial dilator can be used.
-- **Not a substitute for urethroplasty** in recurrent, long, penile, ischemic, or radiation-induced disease — durability is best for short bulbar strictures regardless of dilation method.
+## Limitations and Complications
 
-See also: [Van Buren Sound](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/van-buren), [Dittel Sound](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/dittel), [Balloon Dilator](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/balloon-dilator), [DVIU and Urethral Dilation](/docs/surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/dviu).
+- Cannot traverse a completely obliterated segment without an established tract.
+- False passage or perforation remains possible if the wire itself is misplaced or the dilator is forced.
+- Bleeding, pain, UTI, bacteremia, catheter failure, and loss of access may occur.
+- Sequential dilation does not excise the fibrotic substrate; recurrence is expected in many strictures, especially with adverse length, location, etiology, or prior-treatment features.
+- Repeated early recurrence should prompt reassessment rather than indefinite instrument escalation. The older randomized dilation/urethrotomy study found sharply diminishing durability after repeat treatment for early recurrence.<sup>[[7]](#ref7)</sup>
+
+See also: [Guidewires](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/guidewires), [Balloon Dilator](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/balloon-dilator), [Filiforms & Followers](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/filiforms-followers), and [DVIU and Urethral Dilation](/docs/surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/dviu).
 
